Bottom/top-screen DS ALCHEMY: export the live alchemy state and expose command bridges so the
companion can replace the native AlchemyWindow, which windowmanagerimp already suppresses when the
"game_ui_alchemy" element is DS (companion-hide-gamewindows-on-dsmode.patch — the gate was wired
dormant long before this patch existed).

PRESENTATION + INPUT FORWARDING ONLY. MWMechanics::Alchemy keeps every rule: the shared-effect-by-
>=2-ingredients combination rule AND its slot order (which decides both the created-effect order and
the order effects apply when drunk), the four hardcoded apparatus types, the mortar-and-pestle
requirement, the duplicate-RefId ingredient rejection, the validation order, countPotionsToBrew(),
and the per-potion success roll that consumes the ingredients whatever the outcome.

Three things are load-bearing:

  * The apparatus slots are read from the live mTools, NOT derived. Alchemy::clear() deliberately
    does not clear mTools and the window holds one Alchemy for the whole session, so setAlchemist()
    re-selects whatever the player last hand-picked (while still owned) and only falls back to
    highest quality otherwise. That preference exists nowhere else and cannot be reconstructed.

  * The state batch is emitted from the tail of update(), which the window already calls after EVERY
    mutation (open / apparatus set+clear / ingredient add+clear / create), so the companion never
    tracks anything itself. It is STREAMED one small line per record — the browse list is bounded
    only by how many ingredients the player carries, so a single line would hit the 4096-byte
    stdout-flush truncation.

  * The close signal is emitted explicitly from exit() and onCancelButtonClicked, because in DS mode
    the window is hidden and WindowBase::setVisible() only fires onClose() on a visible->hidden edge
    — so the ordinary onClose() never runs on the real mode-pop. onClose() is still overridden (with
    the usual !containsMode guard, which also suppresses the spurious emit on the DS suppression's
    own transient hide) to cover Vanilla mode. All three funnel through a once-guarded helper.

Unlike LevelupDialog there is deliberately NO exit() refusal: alchemy is genuinely cancellable in
vanilla, so B falls through to Cancel exactly as before and no windowmanagerimp escape-hatch change
is needed.

Result/validation text is routed to the bottom screen in DS mode only (COMPANION_ALCHEMY_MSG, same
shape as COMPANION_CRIME_MSG) because the native transient message renders bottom-centre of the TOP
screen, behind the DS panels. Same GMSTs, same order, same sounds — only the delivery changes.

Two tiny read-only accessors are added to MWMechanics::Alchemy (companionAlchemyFactor /
companionReadyStatus) so the success chance and the ready status shown on screen are the SAME values
createSingle()/getReadyStatus() act on rather than a second implementation.

For a MULTI-POTION batch the DS result line states the counts explicitly ("<brewed> of
<attempted>"). Vanilla cannot express a partial batch -- its success line prints only the brewed
count, and prints the bare singular when exactly one survived, which is indistinguishable from
having asked for one; its failure line carries no count at all, so a wholly-failed batch of four
reads the same as a single failure despite having destroyed four sets of ingredients. Single-attempt
brews keep vanilla's exact wording. Result_NoEffects deliberately carries NO count: create() returns
it before the loop, having consumed exactly one set.

Kotlin side: LogParser prefixes -> AlchemySession in GameStateRepository -> AlchemyOverlay (bottom)
and AlchemyTopOverlay (top). Added Aug 19 2026.

